text:
**Refined prompt:** Trace where a comment's `author` is dropped between request and response in this NestJS RealWorld API, answering as a senior engineer on this codebase, citing file:line for every claim. · Assumptions: you mean the RealWorld `comment.author` object that frontends render as the commenter's name; "the app" = any RealWorld frontend reading `GET /articles/:slug/comments`.

You don't lose it anywhere — it is never captured in the first place. There is no point in the request path where the author exists and then gets dropped; all four layers are missing it independently.

**1. The controller never reads the caller.** `createComment` takes only the slug and body — no `@User()` param, unlike its siblings:

```ts
// src/article/article.controller.ts:77
async createComment(@Param('slug') slug, @Body('comment') commentData: CreateCommentDto) {
```

Contrast `create` at `src/article/article.controller.ts:52`, which does take it: `async create(@User('id') userId: number, ...)`. The JWT *is* there to read — `POST /articles/:slug/comments` is in the guarded list (`src/article/article.module.ts:28`), so `req.user` is populated; the handler just never asks for it.

**2. The DTO has no field for it.** `src/article/dto/create-comment.ts:1-3` is `body` only. Even a client sending `comment.author` has nowhere to land.

**3. The entity has no column and no relation.** `src/article/comment.entity.ts:4-15` — `id`, `body`, and `@ManyToOne` to article. No `author`, no FK to user. So `addComment` sets the one field that exists:

```ts
// src/article/article.service.ts:104-105
const comment = new Comment();
comment.body = commentData.body;
```

**4. The response type declares it away.** The `Comment` interface behind `CommentsRO` is `{ body: string }` — no `author`, no `id`, no `createdAt`:

```ts
// src/article/article.interface.ts:3-5
interface Comment {
  body: string;
}
```

And `findComments` returns the raw eager-loaded entities with no mapping (`src/article/article.service.ts:165-167`), so what ships is `{comments: [{id, body}]}`.

**The fix is a schema change, not a bug fix.** Add `@ManyToOne(() => UserEntity)` + `authorId` to `Comment`, thread `@User('id')` through `createComment` → `addComment`, then build the `author` profile object at the response boundary. This is already on the board — `CURRENT_FOCUS.md` flags that the comment ownership check "needs a `comment.author` relation that does not exist yet," so the same relation unblocks 403-on-delete-someone-else's-comment at `src/article/article.controller.ts:85`.

**What I did not read:** `src/user/user.decorator.ts`, `src/user/user.interface.ts` (`UserData`), the `AuthMiddleware` implementation, and `article.service.ts` lines 1–94 and 130–end beyond the comment methods quoted above. My claim that `req.user` is populated on this route comes from the module config at `article.module.ts:28` plus the knowledge base's auth table, not from reading the middleware.
